{{also|Fragen}}
==German==

===Etymology===
{{dercat|de|gem-pro|ine-pro}}
From {{inh|de|gmh|vrāgen}}, from {{inh|de|goh|frāgēn}}, {{m|goh|frahen|frahēn/frāhēn}}, which is indirectly derived from {{der|de|gmw-pro|*frāgēn}}.<ref>{{R:EWddS|ed=22|hw=Frage, fragen}}</ref> 

Cognate with {{cog|stq|fräigje|t=to ask}}, {{cog|nl|vragen|t=to ask}}, {{cog|en|frain|t=to ask, inquire}}, {{cog|sv|fråga|t=to ask}}, {{cog|is|fregna|t=to hear of, learn about}}.

===Pronunciation===
* {{IPA|de|/ˈfʁaːɡən/|[ˈfʁaːɡŋ̩]}}
* {{audio|de|De-fragen.ogg}}
* {{audio|de|De-fragen2.ogg}}
* {{audio|de|De-fragen3.ogg}}
* {{rhymes|de|aːɡn̩|s=2}}
* {{hyphenation|de|fra|gen}}

===Verb===
{{de-verb|((fragen,fragen<frägt#frug,gefragt,früge.[dialectal or colloquial]>))}}

# {{lb|de|transitive|usually with a second accusative object}} to [[ask]] (someone something)
#: {{ux|de|Darf ich dich etwas '''fragen'''?|May I '''ask''' you something?}}
# {{lb|de|transitive|with {{m|de|nach}}}} to [[ask for]], to [[ask after]]
# {{lb|de|reflexive}} to [[wonder]] (literally, to ask (oneself))
#: {{ux|de|Ich '''frage''' mich, wer mir helfen kann.|I wonder who can help me.}}

====Usage notes====
* Generally, ''fragen'' has always been a weak verb. The strong forms of ''fragen'' are secondary and now obsolete in formal writing. They live on in dialects and colloquial varieties.

